      <!-- 5. Production Hardening & Debugging Guide -->
      <section id="production-hardening" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-8">
        <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-2">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="w-1.5 h-6 bg-link rounded-full"></span>
            Production Hardening: Concurrency, Logging & Trap Prevention
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
            Writing the cron string is only half the battle. Discover the operational best practices needed to keep background jobs stable in production.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
          
          <!-- Overlapping Jobs Problem -->
          <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-6 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2 font-mono">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-link"></span>
              1. Overlapping Execution Prevention with `flock`
            </h3>
            <p>
              If a recurring cron job is scheduled every 5 minutes (<code class="text-white font-mono">*/5 * * * *</code>) but occasionally takes 7 minutes to complete due to network latency, cron will launch a duplicate instance while the first is still running. This frequently causes database race conditions, deadlocks, and memory exhaustion.
            </p>
            <p>
              Use the Linux <code class="text-link font-mono">flock</code> utility to guarantee mutual exclusion (singleton execution):
            </p>
            <div class="bg-canvas-soft-2 p-3 rounded-lg border border-hairline font-mono text-xs text-link overflow-x-auto">
              */5 * * * * /usr/bin/flock -n /var/lock/sync_orders.lock /opt/scripts/sync_orders.sh
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute">
              The <code class="text-white font-mono">-n</code> flag instructs flock to fail non-blockingly and exit immediately if another instance already holds the lock file.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Logging & Redirection -->
          <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-6 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2 font-mono">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-link"></span>
              2. Explicit Standard Output & Error Redirection
            </h3>
            <p>
              By default, if a command executed by cron outputs text or errors to stdout/stderr, the cron daemon attempts to email the output to the local user via the MTA (<code class="text-white font-mono">sendmail</code> or <code class="text-white font-mono">postfix</code>). If an MTA is unconfigured, system mail logs will fill the root disk with delivery failure notices.
            </p>
            <p>
              Always append stdout and stderr to a managed logfile, or discard non-critical output:
            </p>
            <div class="bg-canvas-soft-2 p-3 rounded-lg border border-hairline font-mono text-xs text-link overflow-x-auto">
              # Log stdout and stderr with timestamping:
              0 2 * * * /usr/bin/python3 /opt/backup.py &gt;&gt; /var/log/backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Environment Variables Trap -->
          <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-6 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2 font-mono">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-link"></span>
              3. The Stripped Shell Environment Trap
            </h3>
            <p>
              When running commands interactively in terminal, your session loads environment variables from <code class="text-white font-mono">~/.bashrc</code>, <code class="text-white font-mono">~/.zshrc</code>, or <code class="text-white font-mono">/etc/profile</code>. In stark contrast, <strong>cron invokes commands in a bare, non-interactive shell with an extremely minimal PATH</strong> (often just <code class="text-white font-mono">PATH=/usr/bin:/bin</code>).
            </p>
            <p>
              As a result, commands like <code class="text-white font-mono">node</code>, <code class="text-white font-mono">python</code>, or <code class="text-white font-mono">aws</code> fail with <code class="text-error font-mono">command not found</code>. To resolve this:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-xs font-mono text-body">
              <li>Always define <code class="text-white">PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</code> at the top of your crontab file.</li>
              <li>Use absolute binary paths: <code class="text-white">/usr/bin/node</code> instead of <code class="text-white">node</code>.</li>
              <li>Or source your environment file explicitly: <code class="text-white">* * * * * . /home/app/.env; /opt/app/worker.sh</code>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- Timezone & DST -->
          <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-6 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white flex items-center gap-2 font-mono">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-link"></span>
              4. Daylight Saving Time (DST) & Timezone Shifts
            </h3>
            <p>
              If your host runs on a local timezone observing Daylight Saving Time (e.g. US Eastern or Central European Time), recurring overnight jobs will encounter two critical anomalies every year:
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
              <div class="bg-canvas-soft-2 p-3 rounded border border-hairline space-y-1">
                <span class="text-error font-bold font-mono uppercase">Spring Forward (Skipped Jobs)</span>
                <p>When the clock skips from 02:00 to 03:00, any job scheduled for 02:30 AM will <strong>never execute</strong>.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-canvas-soft-2 p-3 rounded border border-hairline space-y-1">
                <span class="text-warning font-bold font-mono uppercase">Fall Back (Duplicate Jobs)</span>
                <p>When the clock repeats from 02:59 back to 02:00, any job scheduled for 02:30 AM will <strong>run twice</strong>.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-white font-mono">
              ✓ Golden Rule: Configure all production servers and container clusters to Coordinated Universal Time (<code class="text-link font-mono">TZ=UTC</code>).
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

