          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>What is a cron expression and how is it evaluated?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>A cron expression is a compact configuration string consisting of 5 (standard Unix), 6 (Quartz, Spring, AWS), or 7 fields representing discrete divisions of chronological time. Every minute, the background daemon examines the schedule and executes the designated command if all fields match the current system time.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 2 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>What is the critical difference between `* 2 * * *` and `0 2 * * *`?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>This is the single most destructive mistake in cron administration. <code class="text-error font-mono font-bold bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1 rounded">* 2 * * *</code> contains an asterisk in the minute field, meaning <strong>run every single minute during the 2 AM hour</strong> (60 separate executions from 02:00 to 02:59). In contrast, <code class="text-link font-mono font-bold bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1 rounded">0 2 * * *</code> specifies minute 0, running exactly once per day at 02:00 AM.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 3 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>Why is my cron job running on unexpected days when Day-of-Month and Day-of-Week are both specified?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Under the POSIX standard, if both Day of Month and Day of Week are explicitly defined (neither is an asterisk), the scheduler evaluates them using an <strong>OR condition</strong>, not an AND condition! For example, <code class="text-white font-mono">0 0 15 * 5</code> runs on the 15th of the month <em>AND</em> on every Friday of the month. If you need a script to run only when Friday lands on the 15th, you must add a date check inside your execution script.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 4 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>Can standard Unix crontab execute tasks every 30 seconds?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>No. Standard POSIX/Unix cron has a minimum granularity of one minute (60 seconds). To achieve sub-minute scheduling, you can configure two staggered jobs (<code class="text-link font-mono">* * * * * /path/job</code> and <code class="text-link font-mono">* * * * * sleep 30; /path/job</code>), use a 6-field seconds-based engine like Quartz or Node-cron, or employ systemd timer units which support millisecond accuracy.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 5 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>Why does my script work in terminal but fail silently inside Crontab?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>The three most frequent culprits are:</p>
              <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 pl-2">
                <li><strong>Stripped PATH:</strong> Cron operates with a minimal PATH (<code class="text-white font-mono">/usr/bin:/bin</code>). Installed binaries in <code class="text-white font-mono">/usr/local/bin</code> or nvm directories fail to execute.</li>
                <li><strong>Relative Working Directory:</strong> Cron defaults to the user's home folder. Relative paths like <code class="text-white font-mono">./data.csv</code> will resolve incorrectly.</li>
                <li><strong>Missing Newline:</strong> Standard Vixie cron requires crontab files to end with a blank newline. Without it, the final entry is silently ignored.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 6 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>What is the difference between `*` and `?` in cron expressions?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>In standard 5-field Unix cron, <code class="text-white font-mono">?</code> is unsupported and considered a syntax error. However, in 6-field engines like <strong>Quartz</strong> and <strong>AWS EventBridge</strong>, <code class="text-link font-mono">?</code> means "no specific value". It is required in either the Day of Month or Day of Week field when the other field has an explicit value, resolving ambiguity.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 7 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>Is Sunday represented by 0 or 7 in crontab?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>In standard POSIX crontab, Sunday is strictly <code class="text-white font-mono">0</code>. However, most modern Unix implementations (including Linux Vixie Cron, cronie, and CronGen) accept both <code class="text-link font-mono">0</code> and <code class="text-link font-mono">7</code> as Sunday for backward compatibility. In Quartz/Java schedulers, Sunday is represented as <code class="text-white font-mono">1</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 8 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>How do I schedule a job on the last day of every month?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Standard Unix cron has no native symbol for "last day". You can run daily between the 28th and 31st and check if tomorrow is the 1st: <code class="text-link font-mono">59 23 28-31 * * [ $(date -d tomorrow +\%d) -eq 1 ] && /path/to/script.sh</code>. In Quartz and AWS EventBridge, you simply write <code class="text-link font-mono">0 0 0 L * ?</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 9 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>How do I prevent long-running cron jobs from overlapping?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Use file-level descriptor locking via <code class="text-link font-mono">flock -n /var/lock/myjob.lock /path/to/script.sh</code>. The <code class="text-white font-mono">-n</code> argument tells flock to exit cleanly if another instance is currently executing. In Kubernetes, set <code class="text-white font-mono">concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</code> inside your CronJob spec.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 10 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>Why should production cron schedules always run on UTC?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Local timezones that observe Daylight Saving Time (DST) introduce two major annual failures: jobs between 2:00 AM and 3:00 AM are skipped entirely during the spring-forward shift, and executed twice during the fall-back shift. UTC has zero timezone shifts or DST jumps, ensuring uniform intervals year-round.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 11 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>How do I monitor whether my cron job succeeded or failed?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Rather than relying on local server mail, modern DevOps engineers use "dead man's snitch" heartbeats. Services like Healthchecks.io, BetterStack, or Sentry Cron Monitors provide unique webhook URLs that your script pings upon completion: <code class="text-white font-mono">/opt/script.sh && curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 https://hc-ping.com/YOUR-UUID</code>. If the ping is not received on schedule, alerts fire to PagerDuty or Slack.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- FAQ 12 -->
          <details class="group bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong rounded-xl p-4 transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-white text-sm cursor-pointer list-none select-none focus:outline-none">
              <span>What are the security best practices when configuring cron jobs?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-mute group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed border-t border-hairline/50 pt-3 space-y-2">
              <p>Follow the principle of least privilege: never execute application scripts as root. Ensure scripts and log files have restrictive permissions (<code class="text-white font-mono">chmod 700</code>), never pass cleartext API keys or passwords directly as command-line arguments in crontab (they are visible via <code class="text-white font-mono">ps aux</code>), and restrict crontab editing privileges using <code class="text-white font-mono">/etc/cron.allow</code> and <code class="text-white font-mono">/etc/cron.deny</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

        </div>
